---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import GamePlayer from '../../components/GamePlayer.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import games from '../../data/games.json';
import skills from '../../data/skills.json';
import { url } from '../../config';

const game = games.find(g => g.id === 'memory-match-prek')!;
const skill = skills[game.skill as keyof typeof skills];
const page = new URL(url('logic-games/memory-match-for-preschool/'), Astro.site).href;
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'LearningResource', name: `${game.keywordTitle}: ${game.title}`,
    url: page, description: game.summary, isAccessibleForFree: true, inLanguage: 'en',
    learningResourceType: 'Educational game', educationalLevel: 'Preschool and Kindergarten', typicalAgeRange: '4-6',
    teaches: 'Visual memory and matching pairs', timeRequired: 'PT3M', image: new URL(url(game.poster), Astro.site).href,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL(url(), Astro.site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Memory games', item: new URL(url('logic-games/'), Astro.site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: game.keywordTitle, item: page },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Memory Games for Preschool and Kindergarten: Free Matching Pairs Game | EduLearnLabs"
  description="A free memory game for ages 4 to 6. Kids lift quilt flaps to find matching pairs, grow a patchwork quilt and train visual memory. Gentle hints, no ads, no login."
  path="logic-games/memory-match-for-preschool/" nav="logic" image={game.poster} jsonLd={jsonLd}>

  <Breadcrumbs back={{ name: 'Memory games', path: 'logic-games/' }}
    items={[{ name: 'Home', path: '' }, { name: 'Memory games', path: 'logic-games/' }, { name: game.keywordTitle, path: 'logic-games/memory-match-for-preschool/' }]} />

  <h1>Memory Games for Preschool and Kindergarten</h1>
  <p class="lead" style="font-size:20px;max-width:44em">Play <strong>{game.title}</strong>: lift the flaps on a
    patchwork quilt and find two friends that match. Five small boards, about 3 minutes, free.</p>

  <GamePlayer game={game} topDifficulty={skill.topDifficulty}
    posterAlt={`${game.title} game screen: a grid of closed fabric flaps, a small quilt of patches and Hazel the owl`} />

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>What is Quilt Picnic?</h2>
    <p>Quilt Picnic is a gentle matching pairs game set on a sunny meadow picnic. Pictures of friends, like a ladybug,
      a snail, a bee or a rabbit, are hidden under cloth flaps. Your child lifts one flap, then another, and tries to
      find two that match. A match glows, plays a soft note and stays open. Two that do not match simply close again.
      When every pair is found, the board turns into a new patch for your child's quilt.</p>

    <h2>What kids learn</h2>
    <p>The game trains visual memory, the skill of holding a picture in mind and remembering where it was. Children
      also practice looking carefully, comparing two pictures, taking turns with their own attention and trying again
      without getting upset. The boards grow in five small steps:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>2 pairs on a tiny board, a warm-up that nearly everyone wins</li>
      <li>3 and 4 pairs, with pictures that look very different</li>
      <li>6 pairs, where some friends belong to the same family, like two animals</li>
      <li>8 pairs, with friends that look alike, so your child has to look closely</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>Children in preschool and Kindergarten, usually ages 4 to 6. There is nothing to read: a short voice line says
      "Find two that match" and the pictures do the rest. It works best on a tablet, a computer or a phone held sideways,
      and every flap is big enough for small fingers.</p>

    <h2>How to play</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Press Play, then tap the big button to start.</li>
      <li>Tap a flap to lift it, then tap a second flap.</li>
      <li>A match stays open and glows. If the two are different, they close again after a moment.</li>
      <li>Not sure? If your child keeps missing, Hazel the owl lets everyone peek at the board for a second, and later
        one flap twinkles to point at a pair that is still hidden. She never matches a pair for them.</li>
      <li>Find every pair to finish the board and add a patch to the quilt.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Your quilt</h2>
    <p>Every finished board adds one patch to your child's quilt, a small patchwork of gingham, dots, stripes and
      flowers kept on this device. Nothing is sent anywhere. The game counts missed pairs quietly to choose the next
      board, but it never shows a score, a counter or a red mark, and there are no streaks or timers.</p>

    <h2>Why it helps</h2>
    <p>Memory grows with practice that feels like play. Finding pairs asks a child to notice, remember and compare, which
      supports later skills like following directions, learning letters and number facts, and spotting patterns. The
      game starts easy, steps up after a well remembered board and steps back when a board needed a lot of help, so each
      round stays a little tricky and never frustrating.</p>

    <h2>Tips for parents</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Play the first board together and say what you see: "ladybug here, so where is the other ladybug?"</li>
      <li>Missing a lot at first is normal at this age. Cheer the finding, not the speed.</li>
      <li>Short and often works best. One quilt a day is plenty.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">More to play: see all <a href={url('logic-games/')}>free memory games</a>, spell words with
      <a href={url('language-games/word-building-for-grade-1/')}>Word Building for Grade 1</a>, or try
      <a href={url('math-games/addition-for-grade-1/')}>Addition for Grade 1</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <AdSlot id="game-bottom" />
</Base>
